National Bowel Cancer Screening Program Counselling
General Practice Other
Asymptomatic individual 50-74 years, Reluctance/confusion regarding screening, Instruction on collection technique, Discussion of positive result implications

VISIT CONTEXT
Case ID: GPO-028
Reason for Visit: Acute health anxiety and severe emotional distress triggered by receiving a government bowel cancer screening kit, compounded by a recent family death from the disease.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage noted an acutely distressed, elderly patient who experienced a severe panic attack upon receiving the National Bowel Cancer Screening kit in the mail today. The patient’s sibling recently died of metastatic colon cancer, and the arrival of the kit triggered overwhelming fear and a refusal to engage with the health system. Urgent face-to-face assessment was strictly required to physically manage the acute autonomic hyperarousal, provide immediate, compassionate clinical reassurance, and meticulously counsel the patient through the screening process to prevent absolute avoidance. Delaying assessment would have cemented the patient's pathological avoidance and resulted in an unnecessary ED presentation for acute panic tonight.

SUBJECTIVE
Chief Complaint: "I got this test kit in the mail today, my brother died of this last month, and I am absolutely terrified to do it. I can't breathe."
History of Present Illness: The patient is an asymptomatic individual aged between 50 and 74 years, clearly eligible for the population screening program. Following the receipt of the immunochemical faecal occult blood test (iFOBT) kit via mail today, the patient experienced sudden, severe confusion, anxiety, and reluctance regarding the completion of the home-based test. The anxiety escalated into a full panic attack with somatic symptoms including palpitations, shortness of breath, and severe trembling. The patient requires a thorough, calming explanation of the screening rationale targeting early detection of asymptomatic colorectal polyps and early-stage cancer, rather than a definitive diagnosis of terminal illness. A detailed assessment of personal or family history of colorectal cancer confirms a first-degree relative with a recent diagnosis, heavily impacting the psychological presentation.
Functional Status: Independent, but currently exhibiting acute psychomotor agitation and severe anxiety.
Social/Safety Concerns: High risk of pathological avoidance of essential preventative healthcare due to severe, unmanaged grief and fear.

OBJECTIVE
Vitals: BP: 155/95 | HR: 112 | Temp: 36.7 | RR: 22 | SpO2: 99% | Wt: 78 Kg
General: Pacing the room, tearful, hyperventilating, holding the unopened screening kit envelope.
