ASSESSMENT
Primary Diagnosis: National Bowel Cancer Screening Program Counselling (Triggering Acute Health Anxiety) - Status: Stable
Differentials:

Acute Coronary Syndrome - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Tachycardia and chest tightness directly linked to the sudden psychological shock of receiving the kit, with complete resolution of symptoms upon targeted verbal de-escalation and reassurance.

Symptomatic Colorectal Cancer - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Complete absence of rectal bleeding, altered bowel habits, unexplained weight loss, or palpable abdominal masses; the patient is clinically asymptomatic.

Generalized Anxiety Disorder - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The intense panic is entirely situational and specifically triggered by a single, tangible external stimulus (the arrival of the screening kit).
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY
The patient experienced a severe, acute panic attack induced by the traumatic psychological trigger of receiving a cancer screening kit shortly after a sibling's death from the same disease. Telephone triage correctly identified that the acute physiological panic required immediate medical assessment. Urgent face-to-face evaluation was necessary to rule out a cardiac event secondary to the panic, provide immediate psychiatric de-escalation, and utilize the moment to break the cycle of fear and avoidance. By physically opening the kit with the patient, demystifying the process, and framing it as a highly effective, preventative measure, the acute crisis was resolved. Deferring this intervention would have led to an unwarranted ED presentation and the permanent disposal of the life-saving screening kit out of fear.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN
Clinical Interventions: Conducted a thorough physical examination to reassure the patient of their immediate health. Provided extensive verbal de-escalation and grief validation. Opened the iFOBT kit together, providing clear, step-by-step instructions on the proper collection technique. Discussed the high cure rate of early-stage polyps detected by this specific program.
Positive Behavioral Change: To overcome the paralyzing fear of the test, commit to placing the open screening kit directly on the toilet cistern tonight. Do not attempt the test tomorrow. Let it sit there for three days to visually normalize its presence in your bathroom. On the fourth day, execute the simple swab. By slowly habituating to the physical presence of the kit, you drastically lower the anxiety spike associated with it, making the actual completion of the test a manageable, routine action.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None indicated.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Routine GP review in 14 days to confirm the kit was mailed and to follow up on the recent bereavement.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN
Call 000 or present to the nearest Emergency Department if you experience sudden, crushing central chest pain, or if the panic symptoms escalate to a point where you feel entirely unable to breathe despite the reassurance provided.

